[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — out-of-bounds index printed ten zeros instead of crashing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs:** second line in `16..=19` or `26..=27` (e.g. `"1\n16\n"`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C:** killed by SIGSEGV (exit status 139), stdout **empty**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Rust (before):** exit 0, full output ending in `Finished bad()`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The port treated the out-of-bounds write as a discarded no-op, on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assumption that only the ten in-bounds slots are observable. That is wrong: the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m write can land on the frame's control data. `objdump -d` of the binary CMake
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m builds gives the layout exactly (gcc, no optimisation flags):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bad():  sub $0x40,%rsp    buffer          @ rbp-0x40   (10 ints -> rbp-0x19)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           2-byte gap      @ rbp-0x18
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           inputBuffer[14] @ rbp-0x16
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           i               @ rbp-0x8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           data            @ rbp-0x4
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           saved rbp       @ rbp+0x0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           return address  @ rbp+0x8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m main(): sub $0x10,%rsp => main's rbp == bad's rbp + 0x20
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           argc/argv       @ rbp+0x10 .. rbp+0x1f
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           main saved rbp  @ rbp+0x20
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m                           main ret addr   @ rbp+0x28
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `buffer[i]` is at `bad_rbp - 0x40 + 4*i`, so:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | index | target | outcome |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0..=9` | `buffer` | in bounds |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `10..=13` | gap + `inputBuffer` (already consumed) | unobservable |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `14` | `i` — reset to 0 by the very next statement | unobservable |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `15` | `data` — never read again | unobservable |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `16..=17` | `bad()`'s saved rbp | **SIGSEGV** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `18..=19` | `bad()`'s return address | **SIGSEGV** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `20..=23` | `main()`'s `argc`/`argv` — dead | unobservable |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `24..=25` | `main()`'s saved rbp — never dereferenced | unobservable |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `26..=27` | `main()`'s return address | **SIGSEGV** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The write happens *before* the print loop, but the damage is only detected on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ret`, so C prints all ten zeros first and then dies. stdout still comes out
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty because it is fully buffered when it is a pipe, the whole output is far
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m under one buffer, and `exit()` never runs to flush it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `oob_write_is_fatal()` in `src/main.rs` encodes the table above. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m port prints the ten values, then terminates without flushing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — the crash was SIGABRT (134) rather than SIGSEGV (139)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs:** the same ones as mismatch 1, after that fix.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `raise(SIGSEGV)` returned instead of killing the process. Rust's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runtime installs a SIGSEGV handler to turn stack overflow into a clean abort;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for a fault outside the guard page that handler simply returns, so control fell
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through to the `abort()` guard on the next line.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Restore the default disposition first — `signal(SIGSEGV, SIG_DFL)` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then `raise(SIGSEGV)`. Both are declared in an `extern "C"` block; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `*-linux-gnu` targets already link libc, so no new dependency. stderr stays
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m empty, matching C (the `Segmentation fault (core dumped)` line comes from the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m shell, not the process).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 3 — far out-of-bounds indices did not crash
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Inputs:** long lines and large values, e.g. `"11111111111111111111\n"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`bad()` receives the leftover `1111111`) and `"2147483647\n2147483647\n"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C:** killed (139). **Rust (before):** exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Above `main()`'s frame the write walks up through the argv/envp block
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m towards the top of the `[stack]` mapping and faults once it leaves it. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m initial fix stopped modelling faults past index 27 entirely.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Compare the target address against the top of `[stack]` from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `/proc/self/maps`, using the address of the Rust `buffer` as the stand-in for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C's. That reproduces the real mechanism instead of hard-coding a threshold, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it tracks the environment size — see the next section for why that matters.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified stable over repeated runs after the fix: indices `28..=1200` exit 0 on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both, indices `4096 .. 16_777_216` exit 139 on both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Not reproducible by any program: the stack-top boundary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two input classes are excluded from the test suite because **the C program is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not a function of its input there** — the same input, the same binary, gives
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m different answers on different runs. No translation can match them, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m asserting on them would only produce a flaky suite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **(a) Whether the write faults at all**, for indices near the top of the stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mapping. The first faulting index is `(size of argv+envp + ~500) / 4`, and stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ASLR moves the frame relative to the mapping between runs. Measured on the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binary, one index per column, three runs:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         1300  1400  1500  1600  1800  2000  2500  3000
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run 1    139     0     0   139     0     0     0   139
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run 2      0     0     0     0     0     0     0   139
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run 3      0   139     0   139     0   139   139   139
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Growing the environment moves the whole band: with 32 KiB of extra environment,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m index 4096 stops faulting and the boundary shifts out past 16000. Outside the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m band both programs are deterministic and agree — `<= 1200` never faults,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `>= 4096` always does in a normal environment — and those are what the tests
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cover.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **(b) Which signal**, for indices above roughly 6.7e7. There, `4*index` can push
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the address past the x86-64 canonical boundary (`0x0000_7fff_ffff_ffff`), which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reports SIGBUS rather than SIGSEGV, and whether it does depends on the ASLR'd
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack base. `"2222222222222222222222222222222222222222\n"` (`bad()` gets index
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1724130190) over ten runs of the C binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 135 139 135 139 139 139 135 139 135 135      (135 = SIGBUS, 139 = SIGSEGV)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The port raises SIGSEGV, which is the majority outcome, but the split itself is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unmatchable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were verified rather than fixed; they are the places a port would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausibly drift, and the tests pin them down.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` is not `scanf`.** It stops at the newline, keeps it in the buffer,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and takes at most 13 bytes — so `"12345678901234\n"` feeds `goodB2G()` the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   first 13 digits and `bad()` the remainder. A port that read "one line per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   call" would diverge on every line longer than 13 bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` returning NULL** only at EOF-with-nothing-read: empty input prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fgets() failed.` twice; `"5"` with no newline is a successful read, and only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the *next* call fails.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`atoi` is `(int)strtol`.** Leading whitespace (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\f`, `\r`) skipped, one optional sign, digits only, decimal even with leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   zeros (`"007"` is 7), stops at the first non-digit, and truncates on overflow:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"2147483648"` becomes `INT_MIN` and therefore takes the *negative* branch.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"abc"`, `"+"`, `"-"` and `""` all give 0, i.e. index 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **NUL bytes** are copied through by `fgets` and stop `atoi`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Non-UTF-8 input** must not be rejected, which is why the port works in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes throughout rather than in `String`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Output formatting**: `printf("%s\n", ...)` / `printf("%d\n", ...)`, one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   value per line, trailing newline present, nothing on stderr on any path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc`/`argv`** are declared in `main` and never used; arguments change
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   nothing.
